# A composition of the Earth seen from the moon: the lines of thirds, a focus made by the one
# colour, the Earth's size in the moon's sky, and lines that lead the eye.
lesson art-the-earth-from-the-moon v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=8 subject=art levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The Earth from the moon"
  goal "Plan a picture of the Earth seen from the moon so that the eye goes where the artist chooses, using the lines of thirds, colour and leading lines."

  look {
    scene 31x10 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=end {
        earth home up=1
        lander craft rungs=5
        crater holes count=1
      }
    }
    say """
      In December 1968 the crew of Apollo 8, circling the moon in their spacecraft, saw the Earth
      rise over its edge, and the astronaut William Anders took a photograph of it, called
      Earthrise. From the moon's ground the Earth does not rise: it hangs in almost the same place
      in the sky all month. The photograph belongs to everyone, as NASA's pictures do. In it the
      grey moon fills the bottom of the frame, the sky is black, and the small blue Earth is the
      only colour. Planning where the horizon, the Earth and the lines of the ground go is called
      composition, and it decides where the eye goes first.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice art.earth-focus count=1 hard-count=2
    level easy {
      worked art.earth-thirds w=30 h=18 q=0 level=medium
    }
    practice art.earth-thirds count=2 seed=1
    practice art.earth-scale count=2 seed=1
    practice art.earth-contrast count=2 seed=1
    practice art.earth-lead count=1 seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show art.earth-off-centre-why r=1 easy-r=0 hard-r=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show art.earth-rise
  }
  remember {
    say "Put what matters on a line of thirds, make it the most different thing, and lead the eye to it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Earthrise was taken by William Anders on Apollo 8 on 24 December 1968, from lunar orbit: from
    the moon's surface the Earth stays almost fixed in the sky, so only a craft circling the moon
    sees it rise. As a work of NASA it is in the public domain in the United States, and the lesson
    uses drawings of our own. Most of the questions are marked by the machine. The first asks where
    the eye goes in a picture of greys. The core places the horizon and the Earth on the lines of
    thirds, which is fractions of a length, sizes the Earth in the moon's sky against the full moon
    at home, chooses the tone that pulls the eye by its contrast with the tone round it, and chooses
    a line in the ground that leads the eye to the Earth.

    Watch for a child who puts the most important thing in the middle every time, or who sizes the
    Earth like a star. Neither is wrong as a choice, and the two-star question asks what moving the
    Earth onto a third changes. If the thirds are stuck, fold a sheet into three each way and open
    it out. The painting is the three-star question and is responded to rather than marked. Off the
    page, take two photographs of the same thing on a phone, one centred and one on a third, and
    compare them.

    Respond by saying where your own eye went first and how it travelled: "I looked at the Earth
    first, and then followed the footprints back to the lander." Ask what they wanted someone to
    look at.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the eye goes to the Earth, the horizon on a sheet of thirty squares is worked once in the teacher's pen before more horizons, the Earth is sized from the moon, the tones are for the lander on a black sky and a footprint on sunlit ground, the line is the footprints, the two-star is about the Earth, and the three-star is as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the eye goes to the lander and then to a bright horizon, the Earth is placed on the right-hand line and where two lines cross, the moon is sized from the Earth, the tones include the Earth on a dark grey sky, the line is a crater's rim, the two-star is about the horizon, and the three-star is as written."
  }
}
